{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And you shall make upon it a crown of gold round about.</b> Our Sages, of blessed memory (Yoma 72b), said that this is an allusion to the crown of Torah. And concerning the Table and the Altar it says 'and you shall make for it,' because the crown of priesthood and the crown of kingship belong to designated individuals — for priesthood belongs only to one who is of the seed of Aaron, and kingship to the seed of David; therefore it says 'for it.' But the crown of Torah is available to all; therefore it does not say 'for it.' And it employs the term זֵר (crown) to indicate that one who makes use of the crown of Torah — his law is as that of the stranger who comes near, who is put to death. And so regarding the crown of priesthood it is said (Numbers 18:7) 'וְהַזָּר הַקָּרֵב יוּמָת.' And likewise the crown of kingship: a crown for the seed of David, and a stranger (זָר) for strangers." "And as for what is said, 'two cubits and a half its length,' etc. — we have already said that all the cubits were broken, so that every person should imagine in his soul as though he is lacking and has not reached the ultimate of wisdom. And our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Avot 6:6) that the Torah is acquired through forty-eight qualities — an allusion to the matter: 'וְעָשׂוּ אֲרוֹן עֲצֵי שִׁטִּים אַמָּתַיִם וָחֵצִי' — the final letters sum to 396 (קנ״ו). 'אָרְכּוֹ וְאַמָּה וָחֵצִי רָחְבּוֹ וְאַמָּה וָחֵצִי קוֹמָתוֹ' — the final letters sum to 48 (מ״ח), as though it said, 'Acquire the Torah through forty-eight things.' And the allusion comes through broken cubits, because most of the things on which the acquisition of the Torah depends are dependent on the quality of humility, for it is the foundation and source of all those forty-eight things. And likewise the broken cubits allude to a broken and crushed heart, and he is humble and lowly of spirit. And it may be that for this reason the Sage decreed that one should not study in the market, etc., so that people would not accustom themselves to study out of haughtiness, in order to glorify themselves before onlookers — as is the custom of our generation, in which most of those who study make it their way to show off, and in order to multiply their sharpness and dialectics in public."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:22:55.951491148Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}